WHY THIS PROJECT IS GPL
-----------------------

Inner Datum's game engine contains code ported (translated from Lua to
TypeScript) from **T-Engine4 / Tales of Maj'Eyal**:

    Copyright (C) 2009 - 2018 Nicolas Casalini ("DarkGod")
    https://te4.org/license

T-Engine4 is licensed GPL-3.0-or-later.  Translating its source into another
language produces a derivative work, which must carry the same licence when
distributed.  We do that willingly and gratefully — ToME's combat model is the
reason this game feels the way it does.

Note the exact terms.  Every ToME source file carries this header:

    This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
    it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
    the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
    (at your option) any later version.

That final clause is why the correct identifier is **GPL-3.0-or-later**, not
"GPL-3.0-only".  Declaring the latter would silently strip a right upstream
deliberately granted.

Verified by counting, not by sampling: 1,652 of 1,655.lua files in the
reference clone carry the grant.  The 3 that don't (data/lore/arena.lua,
data/talents/spells/infusion.lua, data/zones/keepsake-meadow/zone.lua) carry no
header at all and fall under the project-wide COPYING, which is the verbatim
GPLv3.  No file in the tree mentions GPL version 2.  Upstream's CONTRIBUTING
records that contributors assign copyright to Nicolas Casalini or grant him
relicensing rights, so the grant is made by a single unified rights holder.

Reference clone: https://github.com/vgawen/t-engine4.git
                 commit 304327e0805ca3e2ecdd1c8a6ec2e154bee7411e (2018-09-21)


WHAT THIS COVERS
----------------

Covered by the GPL (derivative of T-Engine4):

  * The turn/energy scheduler
  * Combat resolution: checkHit, rescaleCombatStats, the armour/hardiness
    formula, resistance composition and penetration, the three saves,
    talent-scaling helpers, partial-save duration scaling
  * Status-effect and talent-cooldown machinery
  * Anything else annotated with a `Ported from t-engine4 <path>:<lines>` comment

Also GPL, though written from scratch, because they are part of this program:
everything else under src/, tools/ and test/.  NOTICE § 1.2 and § 1.3 itemise
which is which, so the boundary stays legible to anyone auditing the port.

Per-file headers on ported files read:

    // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
    // Copyright (C) 2026 Dalton Barraclough
    // Ported from t-engine4 game/modules/tome/class/interface/Combat.lua:1444-1462
    // T-Engine4 (C) 2009-2018 Nicolas Casalini "DarkGod" — https://te4.org/license


WHAT THIS DOES *NOT* COVER
--------------------------

**The Outer Index artwork and first-party game content are the author's own
separate work and are NOT licensed under the GPL.**  All Rights Reserved.

That means everything under client/public/assets/ — sprites, tilesets, icons,
UI frames — and the first-party writing under content/{monsters,zones,items,
rules}/, plus the game's name, setting and fiction.  No licence to reuse them
is granted here.

It does NOT mean all of content/.  content/talents/, content/classes/,
content/effects/ and content/maps/ hold values transcribed from T-Engine4's
Lua ( and § 5, which routes TOME/data/timed_effects
into content/effects/*.json, TOME/data/birth/classes into content/classes/
*.json, and ToME's ASCII vaults into content/maps/*.txt — and which says of
talent numbers, "Copy the numbers from the Lua").  Those are derivative works
of a GPL work and are GPL-3.0-or-later.  They are itemised in NOTICE § 1.2.

Claiming All Rights Reserved over transcribed GPL data would be a claim
asserted against upstream's licence, not merely sloppy bookkeeping.  We don't.

This arrangement is explicitly permitted by GPL-3.0 section 5, which provides
that including a covered work in an *aggregate* alongside separate and
independent works "which are not by their nature extensions of the covered
work" does not cause the GPL to apply to those other parts.  Sprites are data
the program consumes, not linked code, and are in no way derived from ToME.

Upstream does exactly the same thing: ToME ships GPL code beside restricted
artwork governed by its own COPYING-MEDIA.  See ASSETS-LICENSE.md for the
asset-side statement.


THE ONE WAY THIS ARRANGEMENT BREAKS
-----------------------------------

The aggregate argument holds only while the art stays in separate files.  If a
build step embeds a PNG into a JavaScript chunk that also contains ported GPL
logic, that chunk is a single combined work, and we would be conveying it under
terms we are unwilling to honour — a violation against DarkGod's code, not
against our own art.  The GPL cannot relicense the author's art; you cannot
infringe your own copyright.  The harm runs upstream, and downstream recipients
get a grant that contradicts itself.

This is not hypothetical.  Vite's `build.assetsInlineLimit` defaults to 4096
bytes; 120 of the 227 UI PNGs in the art source are smaller than that (min
200 B, median 3,456 B).  A default configuration would base64 more than half
the icon set directly into the bundle that carries shared/scale.ts and
shared/checkhit.ts.

Therefore: `build.assetsInlineLimit: 0` in vite.config.ts, and a build
assertion that fails if `data:image` appears in any emitted chunk.  Treat both
as licence compliance, not as build tuning.


THIRD-PARTY ASSETS ACTUALLY IN USE
----------------------------------

  * Fonts — Alegreya Sans, Alegreya Sans SC, Cinzel, IM Fell English.
    SIL Open Font License 1.1.  The OFL_*.txt files ship alongside them and
    must continue to, in every build output.
    None of the four notices declares a Reserved Font Name, so OFL § 3's
    renaming rule does not apply and a modified font may keep its family name.
    Subsetting and WOFF2 conversion are permitted — they produce a "Modified
    Version", which under OFL § 2 must still carry the copyright notice and
    licence, and under § 5 must stay entirely under OFL 1.1 and may not be sold
    on its own.  "Permitted", not "unrestricted".
  * Kenney asset packs — Creative Commons Zero (CC0 1.0).  Used only as
    gap-filler.  Attribution is not required but is given in THIRD_PARTY.md.

  * NOT SHIPPED: the 36 Outer Index music tracks.  The source project records
    no licence file and no provenance note for any of them, and 12 of 26 SFX
    rest on a blanket README claim with no traced original.  They stay out
    until traced.  Do not add assets/audio/ to this repository — the paths are
    gitignored so that re-adding them takes a deliberate override.

See THIRD_PARTY.md for the full ledger.


TALES OF MAJ'EYAL ART AND AUDIO ARE NEVER USED
----------------------------------------------

ToME's graphics, music and sound effects are NOT free.  reference/t-engine4/
COPYING-MEDIA states, verbatim:

    All the medias located in all the "/data/gfx" folders are granted to use
    with the Tales of Maj'Eyal game only.

The local reference clone is Lua source only; it is sparse and never fetches
ToME's media directories, so the exclusion holds by construction rather than by
discipline.  None of that material appears in this project.  All visual assets
come from Outer Index or from the CC0 packs above.

Do not copy anything out of reference/t-engine4 except *code logic*, and cite
it when you do.


TRADEMARKS
----------

The GPL grants rights over code, not names.  "Tales of Maj'Eyal", "T-Engine"
and their logos belong to their owner.  Inner Datum is not affiliated with,
endorsed by, or a product of te4.org.  "Discord" is a trademark of Discord
Inc.; Inner Datum is an unofficial third-party Activity.


OFFER OF SOURCE
---------------

The browser client bundle includes GPL-derived logic (the combat and scaling
functions in shared/), so distributing it to players conveys covered code and
carries the corresponding-source obligation.  It is satisfied by publishing
this repository and linking it from the Activity's About panel.

GPL-3.0 section 6(d) is the governing clause for a web app: offer equivalent
access to the Corresponding Source "in the same way through the same place at
no further charge", with "clear directions next to the object code saying where
to find the Corresponding Source."  In practice that means three things:

  1. A licence banner survives minification (esbuild `--banner:js` / Vite
     `output.banner`) carrying the SPDX line, the upstream copyright and the
     repo URL.
  2. The About panel links the repository — using
     discordSdk.commands.openExternalLink, because a plain <a target="_blank">
     is blocked in the Activity sandbox.
  3. The build's git SHA is embedded and shown, so the source offer points at
     the version actually served rather than at a moving target.

Section 6(d) also makes this durable: "you remain obligated to ensure that it
is available for as long as needed to satisfy these requirements."  Archiving
or privating the repository while friends still have the bundle cached would
put you out of compliance.

Note for the record: GPL-3.0 section 0 provides that "mere interaction with a
user through a computer network, with no transfer of a copy, is not conveying."
Running the server alone would therefore not trigger the obligation — shipping
the client bundle does.  GPLv3 is not the AGPL.  See the design notes for
the compliance checklist.
